O-30..O-33: measure the recognition boundary, and correct two claims it made too strong

Nothing is fixed here. This records what 0.3.4 actually does, because the
question "what exactly does the shipped membrane confine?" could not be
answered from the documents.

The founding promise is the .bio one: what is not granted is unreachable. The
WASM membrane delivers it literally — an ungranted capability is a link error,
so the guest cannot even express the effect. Both membranes we actually ship do
something structurally different: the agent, or the program, keeps full ambient
authority and the membrane reviews the calls it happens to recognise. Under
that model "not in the constitution" stops meaning "impossible" and starts
meaning "no rule matched".

Measured on 0.3.4 with ordinary, idiomatic code — no ctypes, no adversarial
tricks.

O-33, agent membrane. A PowerShell tool call produced NO audit entry at all —
not ALLOW, not DENY, not PASSTHROUGH. The hook was never invoked, because the
installer writes an enumerated matcher (install.py:56, cli.py:371) and that
tool is not on it. Every `metaspace install` ships this. Alongside it,
PASSTHROUGH — a decision category meaning "I did not understand this, so I
allowed it" — cannot exist in a deny-by-default design: there is ALLOW for what
is granted and DENY for everything else, including what is not understood. This
also means our own installer does not satisfy C-02's stated CONDITION, which
requires that the host route EVERY tool effect through the hook.

O-30, program membrane, filesystem. Four of eight idiomatic writes escaped:
io.open, pathlib.Path.write_text, os.open + os.write, os.rename. `pathlib` is
how modern Python writes a file. The cause is structural rather than an
oversight — Python has no chokepoint for file writes, since those are
independent syscall wrappers.

O-31 and O-32 name the two narrow gaps on the surfaces that DO have chokepoints:
socket.getaddrinfo is unmediated, and os.spawn*/os.exec* create a process
without touching Popen. Everything else held: socket, create_connection,
http.client, urllib.urlopen, Popen, subprocess.run, os.system, os.popen were
all refused.

So the useful diagnosis is not "monkeypatching is weak". It works where the
language funnels through one place and cannot work where it does not. C-13 is
therefore true for NETWORK and SUBPROCESS on ordinary code and false for
FILESYSTEM, and it now says so per capability kind.

C-15 is corrected rather than restated. "Bypassable by adversarial native code"
implied that escaping required intent and a lower level; `pathlib` suffices. An
understated limit is worse than a missing one, because it reads as though the
boundary had been examined.

This is the third time this root cause has been written down — O-4 for MCP
vocabularies, O-8 for the apprun monkeypatch, now O-33 for the host's own tool
surface — and the first time it is named as one thing: enumerated recognition
instead of removed authority.

Deliberately not fixed in this commit. Two of the three corrections are product
decisions with real costs: refusing unrecognised tools breaks workflows, and
making the substrate primary narrows what can be promised off Linux. Those are
the maintainer's calls, not the assistant's.

Also of note, on method: the first network probe reported TypeError for
http.client and urllib.urlopen. Writing those up as "passed" would have put two
false fail-opens in the record, at the most important place. Repeated with
clean imports, both are blocked — the fault was in the probe.

Ledger: 51 proven, 6 blocked, 4 planned of 72; 18 open obstacles of 33.
Suite unchanged: 51 proofs, 50 pass + 1 skip. Decision I-56.

